# chrony configuration template - MULE
#
# Configures local time discipline. Time is a security-bearing function here,
# not a convenience: certificate validity, credential expiry and revocation
# freshness all depend on it.
#
# STATUS: TEMPLATE. Every value below is TBD. This file is an input to
# configuration generation, not a file to be edited on a node.
#
# DECISION: FML-ADR-042. Every production hardware block carries a
# battery-backed hardware RTC or equivalent retained local time source. Trust
# validation NEVER FAILS OPEN on invalid time.
#
# Trade: TBR-TIME-01 (holdover, skew tolerance, plausibility, partition rejoin)
#
# THE SOURCE HIERARCHY, from SAD section 24.5.1:
#
#   approved GNSS/NTP source when available
#               |
#             chrony
#               |
#        local MULE system time
#               |
#      peer/local NTP service as required
#               |
#    logs / TAK / certificates / MQTT / audit
#
# GNSS is OPTIONAL MISSION HARDWARE, not a prerequisite for baseline boot.
# WAN time is opportunistic and is NEVER the only time source.

# --- Hardware clock ---------------------------------------------------------
# The RTC is the authority at boot, before any network source exists.
rtcfile /var/lib/chrony/rtc
rtcsync
# TBD: whether the RTC is trusted to set system time unconditionally at boot,
# or only within a plausibility window. See TBR-TIME-01.

# --- Optional GNSS ----------------------------------------------------------
# refclock SHM 0 refid GPS offset TBD delay TBD
# refclock PPS /dev/pps0 lock GPS refid PPS
# TBD: GNSS is optional mission hardware. A node without it must still boot and
# operate. Do not make a refclock a hard dependency.

# --- Peer and upstream sources ----------------------------------------------
# server TBD iburst
# TBD: whether peers on the field mesh serve time to each other, by what rule,
# and whether any node is authoritative. Distribution across a PARTITION is the
# harder half of TBR-TIME-01: two groups out of contact, drifting apart, then
# rejoining, is the normal case for this program.
#
# No upstream server is hardcoded. Local-first means there is usually none.

# --- Skew and step behaviour ------------------------------------------------
# makestep TBD TBD
# TBD: how large a correction may be stepped rather than slewed, and how many
# times. A step during operation moves certificate validity underneath running
# services.

# maxdistance TBD
# TBD: the point beyond which a source is not credible.

# --- Serving time to the local field network --------------------------------
# allow TBD
# FROM THE ADDRESSING SCHEME, not a literal. See TBR-NET-01.
# Serve only the field network. Never a default allow.

# --- TIME_DEGRADED ----------------------------------------------------------
# chrony does not implement this by itself. FML-ADR-042 requires that on boot
# the node evaluate whether retained time is plausible BEFORE trust-sensitive
# operations proceed, and if it is not:
#
#   - enter TIME_DEGRADED;
#   - certificate validity/expiry checks DO NOT FAIL OPEN;
#   - basic local networking may continue where safe;
#   - trust-sensitive enrollment, credential renewal and authoritative service
#     promotion may be restricted;
#   - the operator receives a clear recovery indication.
#
# The last point is a requirement on the Status Aggregator (FML-ADR-046), which
# reports time state so a refusal to validate is diagnosable rather than
# mysterious.
#
# A node with a dead RTC backup cell will refuse to join, in the field, during
# an incident. That is intended. Failing open would make the credential system
# decorative, which is worse than having none.
